Z||000021||DaRkLoRD||12-16-1999||12:32 AM||||my opinion is a bit biased because I'm a kid, but I don't like the way adults used to treat kids (with physical punishment)<P>after hearing some of the stories from my parents of the way they were scared of their parents for fear of being beaten, I often refer to my grandparents' (on my moms side, both deceased..) parenting tactics as slightly fascist. (hope I don't offend anyone) I would never hit a kid for laughing at the table, nor punish a kid for not getting perfect grades in school, etc..<P>However I also think a society in which kids can sue their parents for ridiculous things is crazy. it's gone too far the other way now, and neither way is preferable IMHO.<P>this is slightly more off-topic than I usually get, so I'll stop now.. I just wanted to say the system now doesn't work well enough, but we can't go back to the way things were a couple generations ago now that kids have had a taste of respect. (or freedom, depends on your viewpoint)||||reg||1
Z||000022||Racer 1||12-16-1999||10:58 AM||||Yeah in my hometown we have two highschools one for good kids and one for bad kids. The problem is the bad highschool near where my son goes to pre-school. I thought to myself what a "CHEESY" solution to make another highschool for the bad kids "poor grades,etc". There was a country song named "get over it" the song was funny I cant remeber who sang it though. The US has become a sue happy place were people need to sue other people cause they dont want to work for there $$ or they dont like something that that person does.||||reg||11
Z||000023||olympic||12-16-1999||02:36 PM||||Here in Canada, gun control is very strict. All automatic and assault weapons are illegal. Hand guns are nearly impossible to own(legally). You have to keep them locked in a metal case and you are only allowed to transport them to and from a authorized shooting range. There are also many background checks and licences. A couple of years ago our federal government made a gun registration law. They wan't all gun owners to register all their rifles and shotguns, for a fee of course. It's the first step towards disarming our society. The law even gives the police authority to search your property on the "suspicion" that you have unregistered guns. Unfortunately, many people in this country think that only a criminal would own a gun and support this law. Is a criminal going to register his 9mm?? Is laying a piece of paper next to a shotgun belonging to a law abiding citizen going to prevent crime?? What a joke. Even the police here will tell you(off the record of course) that they think the law is useless and wasting police resources. I think Switzerland has the right idea. All youths a military trained and all households are required by law to have an automatic weapon. Would you kick down someones front door knowing that he owns a machine gun and is trained in how to use it?||||reg||1
Z||000024||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||04:03 PM||||Good point about Switzerland.  For the Americans going to Canada...Leave the firearms at home.  I had a friend who used to go to Canada on a regular basis (another truck driver).  He carried a little .22 auto in his sidebox.  He got his truck inspected in Canada, they found the gun (legally registered in the US) and he went to jail for 18months.  He lost his truck and was banned from Canada for life.  Just a friendly warning!||||reg||1
Z||000025||Patrick||12-16-1999||04:28 PM||||<BR>18 months in jail for trying to bring a gun across the border??!!!<P>I don't know Monkeyman, that sounds a little far-fetched to me. What else was he trying to smuggle into the country?<P>People have spent less time incarcerated here in Canada for killing someone.||||reg||5
Z||000026||Monkeyman||12-16-1999||05:05 PM||||Patrick--The rules aren't the same for Americans in Canada as they are for Canadians in Canada.  He wasn't smuggling anything else into Canada.  The whole time he spent in jail wasn't just his sentence.  He had to wait an unreasonable (IMO) amount of time before he went to trial.  Apparently there's no such thing as "due process" for Americans in Canada.  I still want to know why they even have a border.  Both countries do so much between each other (trade, tourism, etc.) that they should take down the Customs booths and make one country.  It could be called the United States of Canada.  Maybe we would all be called Americadians?  Just a thought.  I'll bet you know some people who claim duality.  I know I do.||||reg||1
